Section 6 : Malware

This section goes over the different types of malware, and includes a module on indications of malware attacks and malware attack techniques.

- The different types of malware are
    Viruses - Malicious files that hide within legit files, commonly seen harming a host system's legitimate files.
    Worms - Self-propogating and laterally moving malware. Stuxnet is an example of a worm.
    Trojans - Malware that disguises itself as legitimate software, think Trojan horse.
    Ransomware - Malware that encrypts files and demands a ransom for a decryption key.
    Rootkits - These are used to deepen and maintain a foothold within a system, allowing for further exploitation of the host.
    Backdoors - A way to enter a host that was previously unknown and has little to no security controls around it. Heartbleed is an old example of a backdoor.
    Logic Bombs - Code embeds that perform malicious activity under certain parameters. A scheduled task that is set to go off at 11:59PM to SSH to an external server is an example.
    Keyloggers - These will record/log your keystrokes in the hopes of capturing password data or other sensitive information you input.
    Spyware - Malware intended to spy on a victim, can be using microphone capture, webcam, etc.
    Bloatware - Malware designed to slow down a system or drain its resources. Think of all the shit that gets downloaded onto your machine if you buy a prebuilt. McAfee, I'm looking at you.


